//! Plan the out-and-back joint move and execute it with every step stamped.
//!
//! The execution protocol is the one `../rust/src/motion.rs` uses (state
//! stream + execution websocket, completion from the state stream through the
//! execution FSM), with two things this app adds:
//!
//! * **`Transport`** -- how the trajectory reaches the controller. `Inline`
//!   sends it in the `InitializeMovementRequest` (what `../rust` does; no
//!   `add_trajectory` REST call, no id known up front). `Cached` calls
//!   `add_trajectory` first and sends the id (what the Python SDK does), which
//!   is the `add_trajectory` phase of the Python twin.
//! * **`StateStream`** -- whether the motion-group state websocket is opened
//!   per execution (`reopen`) or once for the run (`persistent`). There is no
//!   shared pump in the Rust client, so `linger` has no counterpart here.
//!
//! Every stamp goes into a `CycleTiming` (see `timing.rs`).

use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

use anyhow::{anyhow, bail, Context, Result};
